cff-version: 1.2.0
title: 'Cosmological Voids vs Temporal Shear: An Empirical Falsification of Kinematic Hubble Tension Solutions'
message: If you use this software, please cite it as below.
type: article
authors:
- family-names: Smawfield
  given-names: Matthew Lukin
  orcid: https://orcid.org/0009-0003-8219-3159
abstract: 'Recent literature proposes that the 5-sigma Hubble tension can be resolved
  by macroscopic kinematic outflows driven by a Gpc-scale local underdensity (the
  KBC void) operating under Milgromian dynamics (MOND). A fundamental requirement
  of any purely kinematic resolution is the uniform scaling of all local photometric
  distance indicators. This paper delivers a statistically decisive rejection of
  the published KBC gradual-decay predictions on the data and covariance the void
  authors themselves rely upon, and presents two supporting diagnostics. The primary
  falsification uses the unbinned native mu-space likelihood with the native 1701x1701
  Pantheon+ STAT+SYS release covariance, restricted to the matched 1053x1053 submatrix
  at z >= 0.05 and a marginalized common zero-point, rejecting the
  published KBC Method-3 curves over the model''s stated validity domain (z >= 0.05)
  with Delta-AIC = +101.5 (Gaussian) and +117.1 (Exponential) at the KBC-preferred
  reference Omega_m = 0.302; extending to the full Pantheon+ range strengthens the
  rejection to +194.3 and +328.7. The rejection is robust to digitization uncertainty
  (±1.0 km/s/Mpc leaves Delta-AIC > 99 at z >= 0.05 and > 190 over the full release).
  The calibration-independent relative test indicates that H0 does not decline from
  0.05 <= z < 0.15 to z > 0.25 (R_H = 1.0066 ± 0.00642, 8.4sigma from the KBC Gaussian
  prediction R_H = 0.9524). Two supporting diagnostics reinforce this: (i) indicator-specific
  distance divergence (2.39sigma one-sided sign test from 17/22 galaxies; Xi regression
  negative on the pre-specified primary analysis and independent CF4 non-R22 subset;
  CF4 full-sample positive slope shown by exclusion test to be driven by two galaxies
  with known large differential registration shifts in CF4 data; kappa_Cep = (0.400
  ± 0.270)x10^6 mag from the joint multi-block likelihood), and (ii) peculiar velocity
  calibration sensitivity (3.3% bulk-flow amplitude change under Cepheid-to-TRGB
  recalibration). The framework is grounded in the Temporal Equivalence Principle
  (TEP), a scalar-tensor framework in which proper time operates as a dynamical field
  coupled to the local gravitational potential. The Hubble tension is not a kinematic
  void effect; it is a clock-calibration bias.'
